WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.301
The Last Leaf, by O. Henry.

00:00:02.301 --> 00:00:10.560
In a little district west of Washington Square the streets have run crazy and broken themselves into small strips called “places.”

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.326
These “places” make strange angles and curves.

00:00:14.326 --> 00:00:17.435
One street crosses itself a time or two.

00:00:17.435 --> 00:00:21.757
An artist once discovered a valuable possibility in this street.

00:00:21.757 --> 00:00:32.534
Suppose a collector with a bill for paints, paper and canvas should, in traversing this route, suddenly meet himself coming back, without a cent having been paid on account!

00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:42.860
So, to quaint old Greenwich Village the art people soon came prowling, hunting for north windows and eighteenth-century gables and Dutch attics and low rents.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:49.994
Then they imported some pewter mugs and a chafing dish or two from Sixth avenue, and became a “colony.”

00:00:49.994 --> 00:00:55.499
At the top of a squatty, three-story brick Sue and Johnsy had their studio.

00:00:55.499 --> 00:00:58.234
“Johnsy” was familiar for Joanna.

00:00:58.234 --> 00:01:01.330
One was from Maine; the other from California.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:12.117
They had met at the table d’hôte of an Eighth street “Delmonico’s,” and found their tastes in art, chicory salad and bishop sleeves so congenial that the joint studio resulted.

00:01:12.117 --> 00:01:13.325
That was in May.

00:01:13.325 --> 00:01:23.089
In November a cold, unseen stranger, whom the doctors called Pneumonia, stalked about the colony, touching one here and there with his icy fingers.

00:01:23.089 --> 00:01:34.326
Over on the east side this ravager strode boldly, smiting his victims by scores, but his feet trod slowly through the maze of the narrow and moss-grown “places.”

00:01:34.326 --> 00:01:34.736
Mr.

00:01:34.736 --> 00:01:38.830
Pneumonia was not what you would call a chivalric old gentleman.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:47.335
A mite of a little woman with blood thinned by California zephyrs was hardly fair game for the red-fisted, short-breathed old duffer.

00:01:47.335 --> 00:01:58.289
But Johnsy he smote; and she lay, scarcely moving, on her painted iron bedstead, looking through the small Dutch window-panes at the blank side of the next brick house.

00:01:58.289 --> 00:02:04.167
One morning the busy doctor invited Sue into the hallway with a shaggy, gray eyebrow.

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:12.025
“She has one chance in—let us say, ten,” he said, as he shook down the mercury in his clinical thermometer.

00:02:12.025 --> 00:02:14.342
“And that chance is for her to want to live.

00:02:14.342 --> 00:02:20.944
This way people have of lining-up on the side of the undertaker makes the entire pharmacopeia look silly.

00:02:20.944 --> 00:02:25.195
Your little lady has made up her mind that she’s not going to get well.

00:02:25.195 --> 00:02:27.027
Has she anything on her mind?”

00:02:27.027 --> 00:02:31.740
“She—she wanted to paint the Bay of Naples some day,” said Sue.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.181
“Paint?—bosh!

00:02:33.181 --> 00:02:38.586
Has she anything on her mind worth thinking about twice—a man, for instance?”

00:02:38.586 --> 00:02:43.388
“A man?” said Sue, with a jew’s-harp twang in her voice.

00:02:43.388 --> 00:02:48.287
“Is a man worth—but, no, doctor; there is nothing of the kind.”

00:02:48.287 --> 00:02:51.990
“Well, it is the weakness, then,” said the doctor.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:57.942
“I will do all that science, so far as it may filter through my efforts, can accomplish.

00:02:57.942 --> 00:03:07.201
But whenever my patient begins to count the carriages in her funeral procession I subtract 50 per cent. from the curative power of medicines.

00:03:07.201 --> 00:03:16.704
If you will get her to ask one question about the new winter styles in cloak sleeves I will promise you a one-in-five chance for her, instead of one in ten.”

00:03:16.704 --> 00:03:22.738
After the doctor had gone Sue went into the workroom and cried a Japanese napkin to a pulp.

00:03:22.738 --> 00:03:28.114
Then she swaggered into Johnsy’s room with her drawing board, whistling ragtime.

00:03:28.114 --> 00:03:34.553
Johnsy lay, scarcely making a ripple under the bedclothes, with her face toward the window.

00:03:34.553 --> 00:03:37.752
Sue stopped whistling, thinking she was asleep.

00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:44.129
She arranged her board and began a pen-and-ink drawing to illustrate a magazine story.

00:03:44.129 --> 00:03:52.728
Young artists must pave their way to Art by drawing pictures for magazine stories that young authors write to pave their way to Literature.

00:03:52.728 --> 00:04:03.754
As Sue was sketching a pair of elegant horseshow riding trousers and a monocle on the figure of the hero, an Idaho cowboy, she heard a low sound, several times repeated.

00:04:03.754 --> 00:04:06.019
She went quickly to the bedside.

00:04:06.019 --> 00:04:08.375
Johnsy’s eyes were open wide.

00:04:08.375 --> 00:04:12.128
She was looking out the window and counting—counting backward.

00:04:12.128 --> 00:04:22.298
“Twelve,” she said, and a little later “eleven;” and then “ten,” and “nine;” and then “eight” and “seven,” almost together.

00:04:22.298 --> 00:04:25.189
Sue looked solicitously out the window.

00:04:25.189 --> 00:04:26.660
What was there to count?

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:33.392
There was only a bare, dreary yard to be seen, and the blank side of the brick house twenty feet away.

00:04:33.392 --> 00:04:40.033
An old, old ivy vine, gnarled and decayed at the roots, climbed half way up the brick wall.

00:04:40.033 --> 00:04:48.855
The cold breath of autumn had stricken its leaves from the vine until its skeleton branches clung, almost bare, to the crumbling bricks.

00:04:48.855 --> 00:04:51.157
“What is it, dear?” asked Sue.

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:54.542
“Six,” said Johnsy, in almost a whisper.

00:04:54.542 --> 00:04:56.233
“They’re falling faster now.

00:04:56.233 --> 00:04:58.882
Three days ago there were almost a hundred.

00:04:58.882 --> 00:05:01.082
It made my head ache to count them.

00:05:01.082 --> 00:05:02.478
But now it’s easy.

00:05:02.478 --> 00:05:03.882
There goes another one.

00:05:03.882 --> 00:05:05.909
There are only five left now.”

00:05:05.909 --> 00:05:07.428
“Five what, dear.

00:05:07.428 --> 00:05:08.579
Tell your Sudie.”

00:05:08.579 --> 00:05:09.435
“Leaves.

00:05:09.435 --> 00:05:10.742
On the ivy vine.

00:05:10.742 --> 00:05:13.778
When the last one falls I must go, too.

00:05:13.778 --> 00:05:15.807
I’ve known that for three days.

00:05:15.807 --> 00:05:17.451
Didn’t the doctor tell you?”

00:05:17.451 --> 00:05:23.165
“Oh, I never heard of such nonsense,” complained Sue, with magnificent scorn.

00:05:23.165 --> 00:05:26.237
“What have old ivy leaves to do with your getting well?

00:05:26.237 --> 00:05:30.050
And you used to love that vine so, you naughty girl.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:31.375
Don’t be a goosey.

00:05:31.375 --> 00:05:41.365
Why, the doctor told me this morning that your chances for getting well real soon were—let’s see exactly what he said—he said the chances were ten to one!

00:05:41.365 --> 00:05:48.631
Why, that’s almost as good a chance as we have in New York when we ride on the street cars or walk past a new building.

00:05:48.631 --> 00:05:59.724
Try to take some broth now, and let Sudie go back to her drawing, so she can sell the editor man with it, and buy port wine for her sick child, and pork chops for her greedy self.”

00:05:59.724 --> 00:06:05.215
“You needn’t get any more wine,” said Johnsy, keeping her eyes fixed out the window.

00:06:05.215 --> 00:06:06.462
“There goes another.

00:06:06.462 --> 00:06:08.602
No, I don’t want any broth.

00:06:08.602 --> 00:06:10.169
That leaves just four.

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:13.769
I want to see the last one fall before it gets dark.

00:06:13.769 --> 00:06:15.507
Then I’ll go, too.”

00:06:15.507 --> 00:06:24.599
“Johnsy, dear,” said Sue, bending over her, “will you promise me to keep your eyes closed, and not look out the window until I am done working?

00:06:24.599 --> 00:06:27.431
I must hand those drawings in by to-morrow.

00:06:27.431 --> 00:06:30.649
I need the light, or I would draw the shade down.”

00:06:30.649 --> 00:06:34.973
“Couldn’t you draw in the other room?” asked Johnsy, coldly.

00:06:34.973 --> 00:06:37.560
“I’d rather be here by you,” said Sue.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.833
“Besides I don’t want you to keep looking at those silly ivy leaves.”

00:06:41.833 --> 00:06:51.595
“Tell me as soon as you have finished,” said Johnsy, closing her eyes, and lying white and still as a fallen statue, “because I want to see the last one fall.

00:06:51.595 --> 00:06:52.976
I’m tired of waiting.

00:06:52.976 --> 00:06:54.452
I’m tired of thinking.

00:06:54.452 --> 00:07:02.212
I want to turn loose my hold on everything, and go sailing down, down, just like one of those poor, tired leaves.”

00:07:02.811 --> 00:07:04.928
“Try to sleep,” said Sue.

00:07:04.928 --> 00:07:09.169
“I must call Behrman up to be my model for the old hermit miner.

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:10.764
I’ll not be gone a minute.

00:07:10.764 --> 00:07:13.001
Don’t try to move ’till I come back.”

00:07:13.001 --> 00:07:17.486
Old Behrman was a painter who lived on the ground floor beneath them.

00:07:17.486 --> 00:07:25.319
He was past sixty and had a Michael Angelo’s Moses beard curling down from the head of a satyr along the body of an imp.

00:07:25.319 --> 00:07:27.588
Behrman was a failure in art.

00:07:27.588 --> 00:07:33.923
Forty years he had wielded the brush without getting near enough to touch the hem of his Mistress’s robe.

00:07:33.923 --> 00:07:38.815
He had been always about to paint a masterpiece, but had never yet begun it.

00:07:38.815 --> 00:07:45.470
For several years he had painted nothing except now and then a daub in the line of commerce or advertising.

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:52.615
He earned a little by serving as a model to those young artists in the colony who could not pay the price of a professional.

00:07:52.615 --> 00:07:57.401
He drank gin to excess, and still talked of his coming masterpiece.

00:07:57.401 --> 00:08:10.117
For the rest he was a fierce little old man, who scoffed terribly at softness in any one, and who regarded himself as especial mastiff-in-waiting to protect the two young artists in the studio above.

00:08:10.117 --> 00:08:16.368
Sue found Behrman smelling strongly of juniper berries in his dimly lighted den below.

00:08:16.368 --> 00:08:24.762
In one corner was a blank canvas on an easel that had been waiting there for twenty-five years to receive the first line of the masterpiece.

00:08:24.762 --> 00:08:35.980
She told him of Johnsy’s fancy, and how she feared she would, indeed, light and fragile as a leaf herself, float away when her slight hold upon the world grew weaker.

00:08:35.980 --> 00:08:43.520
Old Behrman, with his red eyes plainly streaming, shouted his contempt and derision for such idiotic imaginings.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:45.146
“Vass!” he cried.

00:08:45.146 --> 00:08:52.323
“Is dere people in de world mit der foolishness to die because leafs dey drop off from a confounded vine?

00:08:52.323 --> 00:08:54.500
I haf not heard of such a thing.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:59.204
No, I will not bose as a model for your fool hermit-dunderhead.

00:08:59.204 --> 00:09:04.181
Vy do you allow dot silly pusiness to come in der prain of her?

00:09:04.181 --> 00:09:07.081
Ach, dot poor lettle Miss Johnsy.”

00:09:07.081 --> 00:09:14.380
“She is very ill and weak,” said Sue, “and the fever has left her mind morbid and full of strange fancies.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:15.605
Very well, Mr.

00:09:15.605 --> 00:09:19.502
Behrman, if you do not care to pose for me, you needn’t.

00:09:19.502 --> 00:09:22.878
But I think you are a horrid old—old flibbertigibbet.”

00:09:22.878 --> 00:09:25.519
“You are just like a woman!” yelled Behrman.

00:09:25.519 --> 00:09:27.464
“Who said I will not bose?

00:09:27.464 --> 00:09:28.266
Go on.

00:09:28.266 --> 00:09:30.067
I come mit you.

00:09:30.067 --> 00:09:34.768
For half an hour I haf peen trying to say dot I am ready to bose.

00:09:34.768 --> 00:09:40.987
Gott! dis is not any blace in which one so goot as Miss Yohnsy shall lie sick.

00:09:40.987 --> 00:09:45.698
Some day I vill baint a masterpiece, and ve shall all go away.

00:09:45.698 --> 00:09:47.129
Gott! yes.”

00:09:47.129 --> 00:09:50.121
Johnsy was sleeping when they went upstairs.

00:09:50.121 --> 00:09:55.670
Sue pulled the shade down to the window-sill, and motioned Behrman into the other room.

00:09:55.670 --> 00:09:59.742
In there they peered out the window fearfully at the ivy vine.

00:09:59.742 --> 00:10:02.919
Then they looked at each other for a moment without speaking.

00:10:02.919 --> 00:10:07.014
A persistent, cold rain was falling, mingled with snow.

00:10:07.014 --> 00:10:13.696
Behrman, in his old blue shirt, took his seat as the hermit-miner on an upturned kettle for a rock.

00:10:13.696 --> 00:10:22.084
When Sue awoke from an hour’s sleep the next morning she found Johnsy with dull, wide-open eyes staring at the drawn green shade.

00:10:22.084 --> 00:10:26.296
“Pull it up; I want to see,” she ordered, in a whisper.

00:10:26.296 --> 00:10:27.977
Wearily Sue obeyed.

00:10:28.592 --> 00:10:38.762
But, lo! after the beating rain and fierce gusts of wind that had endured through the livelong night, there yet stood out against the brick wall one ivy leaf.

00:10:38.762 --> 00:10:40.710
It was the last on the vine.

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:51.760
Still dark green near its stem, but with its serrated edges tinted with the yellow of dissolution and decay, it hung bravely from a branch some twenty feet above the ground.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:54.272
“It is the last one,” said Johnsy.

00:10:54.272 --> 00:10:56.898
“I thought it would surely fall during the night.

00:10:56.898 --> 00:10:58.047
I heard the wind.

00:10:58.047 --> 00:11:01.834
It will fall to-day, and I shall die at the same time.”

00:11:01.834 --> 00:11:09.530
“Dear, dear!” said Sue, leaning her worn face down to the pillow, “think of me, if you won’t think of yourself.

00:11:09.530 --> 00:11:10.571
What would I do?”

00:11:10.571 --> 00:11:12.722
But Johnsy did not answer.

00:11:12.722 --> 00:11:19.648
The lonesomest thing in all the world is a soul when it is making ready to go on its mysterious, far journey.

00:11:19.648 --> 00:11:27.009
The fancy seemed to possess her more strongly as one by one the ties that bound her to friendship and to earth were loosed.

00:11:27.009 --> 00:11:34.404
The day wore away, and even through the twilight they could see the lone ivy leaf clinging to its stem against the wall.

00:11:34.404 --> 00:11:43.536
And then, with the coming of the night the north wind was again loosed, while the rain still beat against the windows and pattered down from the low Dutch eaves.

00:11:43.536 --> 00:11:49.026
When it was light enough Johnsy, the merciless, commanded that the shade be raised.

00:11:49.026 --> 00:11:51.227
The ivy leaf was still there.

00:11:51.227 --> 00:11:54.106
Johnsy lay for a long time looking at it.

00:11:54.106 --> 00:11:59.354
And then she called to Sue, who was stirring her chicken broth over the gas stove.

00:11:59.354 --> 00:12:02.517
“I’ve been a bad girl, Sudie,” said Johnsy.

00:12:02.517 --> 00:12:07.371
“Something has made that last leaf stay there to show me how wicked I was.

00:12:07.371 --> 00:12:09.347
It is a sin to want to die.

00:12:09.347 --> 00:12:21.043
You may bring me a little broth now, and some milk with a little port in it, and—no; bring me a hand-mirror first, and then pack some pillows about me, and I will sit up and watch you cook.”

00:12:21.043 --> 00:12:22.846
An hour later she said.

00:12:22.846 --> 00:12:26.702
“Sudie, some day I hope to paint the Bay of Naples.”

00:12:26.702 --> 00:12:32.554
The doctor came in the afternoon, and Sue had an excuse to go into the hallway as he left.

00:12:32.554 --> 00:12:38.096
“Even chances,” said the doctor, taking Sue’s thin, shaking hand in his.

00:12:38.096 --> 00:12:39.996
“With good nursing you’ll win.

00:12:39.996 --> 00:12:43.329
And now I must see another case I have downstairs.

00:12:43.329 --> 00:12:47.871
Behrman, his name is—some kind of an artist, I believe.

00:12:47.871 --> 00:12:49.299
Pneumonia, too.

00:12:49.299 --> 00:12:53.214
He is an old, weak man, and the attack is acute.

00:12:53.214 --> 00:12:58.550
There is no hope for him; but he goes to the hospital to-day to be made more comfortable.”

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:02.867
The next day the doctor said to Sue: “She’s out of danger.

00:13:02.867 --> 00:13:03.720
You’ve won.

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:06.596
Nutrition and care now—that’s all.”

00:13:06.596 --> 00:13:18.448
And that afternoon Sue came to the bed where Johnsy lay, contentedly knitting a very blue and very useless woolen shoulder scarf, and put one arm around her, pillows and all.

00:13:19.048 --> 00:13:22.664
“I have something to tell you, white mouse,” she said.

00:13:22.664 --> 00:13:23.084
“Mr.

00:13:23.084 --> 00:13:26.285
Behrman died of pneumonia to-day in the hospital.

00:13:26.285 --> 00:13:28.424
He was ill only two days.

00:13:28.424 --> 00:13:34.326
The janitor found him on the morning of the first day in his room downstairs helpless with pain.

00:13:34.326 --> 00:13:37.743
His shoes and clothing were wet through and icy cold.

00:13:37.743 --> 00:13:41.546
They couldn’t imagine where he had been on such a dreadful night.

00:13:41.546 --> 00:13:56.815
And then they found a lantern, still lighted, and a ladder that had been dragged from its place, and some scattered brushes, and a palette with green and yellow colors mixed on it, and—look out the window, dear, at the last ivy leaf on the wall.

00:13:56.815 --> 00:14:01.117
Didn’t you wonder why it never fluttered or moved when the wind blew?

00:14:01.117 --> 00:14:07.513
Ah, darling, it’s Behrman’s masterpiece—he painted it there the night that the last leaf fell.”
